---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Progress from '../../components/Progress.astro';
import PersonHeader from '../../components/PersonHeader.astro';
const sections = [
  { id: 'key', label: 'Key points' }, { id: 'do', label: 'Do this' },
  { id: 'detail', label: 'In detail' }, { id: 'links', label: 'Links' },
];
---
<Base title="Abhik Sarkar" description="Abhik's view of the AI infra market and how to learn it." brand="nvidia" current="people">
<Progress plan="person-abhik" sections={sections} />
<PersonHeader slug="abhik" />

<h2 id="key"><span class="num">1</span>Key points</h2>
<div class="card">
<div class="q"><b>1. Big talent gap, and it looks like early cloud.</b><span>Like cloud around 2010–2015, ops know-how barely exists yet. The cycle will be shorter. DevOps is saturated; LLM/AI infra "is empty."</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>2. Two hard skills: performance engineering and networking.</b><span>AI networks run at 400 Gbps, where 100G is the fallback, not the gold standard.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>3. Start from what you already understand.</b><span>The vLLM blog will feel foreign; the llm-d blog (networking, routers, latency, 100G vs 400G) will feel familiar. Grow from there.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>4. Study the workload, not the chip.</b><span>First homework: GPU workloads vs CPU workloads at scale, and why the world is moving. Distributed computing is the key, not memorizing H100 specs.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>5. 4–5 months of deep study, applying in parallel.</b><span>"It's not research at all, it's operations." You can learn on the job.</span></div>
</div>

<h2 id="do"><span class="num">2</span>Do this</h2>
<div class="week"><h3>Abhik's actions <span>tick as you go</span></h3>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab1"><label for="ab1">Homework 1: write 1 page on GPU vs CPU workloads at scale: power (~600 W vs ~140 W), failure rates, distributed sync, why one slow GPU slows all.<span class="done-when">Done when you can explain it without mentioning matrix multiplication.</span></label></div>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab2"><label for="ab2">Read the llm-d post "Networking for distributed inference", highlighting only the terms you already know, then look up the rest.<span class="done-when">Done when every term on the page is in your glossary.</span></label></div>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab3"><label for="ab3">Study ClusterMAX: how GPU clouds are rated (gold/platinum…) and what separates the best from AWS-style clouds (topology, reliability).<span class="done-when">Done when you can name 3 things that make a GPU cloud "platinum".</span></label></div>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab4"><label for="ab4">Design his starter case: run DeepSeek (or another open model) in-house. Cover topology, max tokens/sec, fault tolerance, scaling, quotas for internal teams.<span class="done-when">Done when it's a 1-page design with a scaling plan.</span></label></div>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab5"><label for="ab5">Do the $80K/month question: when does buying GPUs (~$1M capex) beat paying API token bills? Include semantic routing to the API as overflow.<span class="done-when">Done when you have a break-even month and a recommendation.</span></label></div>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab6"><label for="ab6">Use deep research (ChatGPT/Claude) to find 3 well-structured Substack series on "DevOps → LLM/AI infra engineer", e.g. GPU vs CPU → prefill vs decode → why networking matters.<span class="done-when">Done when 3 series are bookmarked and one is finished.</span></label></div>
<div class="task"><input type="checkbox" id="ab7"><label for="ab7">Send him your finalized roadmap link for a 5-minute review (he said message him as often as needed).<span class="done-when">Done when sent.</span></label></div>
</div>

<h2 id="detail"><span class="num">3</span>In detail</h2>
<div class="card">
<b>Where the industry is going</b>
<div class="q"><b>Move to in-house GPUs</b><span>Companies are moving from external providers to their own GPUs for <b>availability</b> and <b>cost</b>. Texas is "the capital of data centers" right now.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Open models + routing</b><span>Many companies use Anthropic/OpenAI today, but decent open models exist (DeepSeek, Kimi, GLM). Pattern being evaluated: reserved/on-prem capacity first, <b>semantic routing</b>, then fall back to the API. Teams also collect traces (e.g. LangSmith) to fine-tune their own models. Still early and driven by cost and volume.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Supply</b><span>GPUs are scarce and chaotic now, likely for ~6 months; memory makers are earning a lot. GPUs get more expensive over time, so buying or long contracts make sense, unlike cars or CPUs that depreciate ("like gold").</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Capex now, jobs next</b><span>The billions you see are capex: land and GPUs still being built. Roles open once the infra is half-built; by end-2027 a lot will be ready to operate.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Neoclouds</b><span>GPU-only clouds such as CoreWeave, TensorWave and Together AI; ~115 in the US (his estimate), hiring heavily. Big companies building their own capacity is why CoreWeave's stock dipped. Many data centers sit outside big cities around DFW and want local talent; one he spoke to outsourced network engineering to Italy.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Why AWS isn't the leader for training</b><span>GPU topology matters: two GPUs physically close are far faster than far-apart ones, and AWS's allocation historically didn't care. That shows up in ClusterMAX ratings.</span></div>
</div>
<div class="card">
<b>What the job is</b>
<div class="q"><b>Why it pays</b><span>An NVL72 rack costs $3–4M; 72 GPUs at ~$4/GPU-hour is a big hourly bill. Companies pay a lot to keep it running smoothly, recover fast and maximize utilization. CPUs break every 6–12 months; dense GPUs break far more often.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Daily problems</b><span>Where things stall and break. Example: six GPUs synchronizing, one slower, so all wait. ML engineers say "I deployed, the network is saturated"; DevOps says "that's your app." Someone who can own both is missing.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Two kinds of roles</b><span><b>In-house</b> (bank or large corp): an AI infra team that AI engineers go through. You decide how to deploy on many machines, topology, bottlenecks, time-slicing, quotas. You're the architect. <b>Neoclouds:</b> consulting for smaller companies plus running their own fleet.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Gold standard for on-prem</b><span>Maximum tokens per second, kept continuously, and fault tolerance when something fails. Without NVL72, GPUs talk over InfiniBand, so learn what InfiniBand is and gang scheduling.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Configs are the value</b><span>Developers can write configs but rarely understand their impact or bottlenecks. Neoclouds and hyperscalers lack this know-how and are pushed for better utilization and faster recovery.</span></div>
</div>
<div class="card">
<b>How to learn</b>
<div class="q"><b>Access is the gatekeeper</b><span>Without machines it's a chicken-and-egg problem. NVIDIA runs upskilling and certifications.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Text over video</b><span>Substack has many free follow-alongs; YouTube is slower because detailed videos have small audiences. He uses ChatGPT/Codex deep research to find Substack articles, with detailed prompts. He named a woman who teaches a Maven course whose articles are "pretty good". From the description this is very likely <a href="../abi-aryan/">Abi Aryan</a>.</span></div>
<div class="q"><b>Pace</b><span>Learn concepts close to what you know first; jumping into GPU internals burns you out. He shared his own "AI infra upskilling roadmap" link in chat (orchestration and workload management with Slurm, advanced networking) and offered a call summary by email.</span></div>
</div>

